CLICKDEAD-xhci — v2 — the guard's SILENT exit, counted as TWO populations.
==========================================================================

**v2 supersedes v1.** rmbp 12 GRANTED `xhci/mod.rs` on 2026-09-06 with two binding
conditions, and this revision is what they produce:

  1. **SPLIT the counter.** v1's single `MOUSE_DUP_DROP_COUNT`, bumped on
     `param == prev || !have_buf`, CONFLATED two failures that need opposite repairs.
     v2 declares a second ungated atomic, `MOUSE_NOBUF_DROP_COUNT`, and the guard's exit
     scores exactly one of them. See "THE SPLIT" below.
  2. **The counters stay UNGATED, in the house pattern of their siblings.** The pattern is
     `80ed35a4:MOUSE_REARM_COUNT`'s doc comment (`drivers/xhci/mod.rs:2373-2377`): *"Bumped
     unconditionally (cheap relaxed adds); only the knob-gated witness prints."* The two new
     statics join `80ed35a4:MOUSE_REARM_COUNT` (`:2377`),
     `80ed35a4:MOUSE_DISCARD_REARM_COUNT` (`:2380`) and `80ed35a4:MOUSE_ERROR_REARM_COUNT`
     (`:2385`) on that rule. That is what moves the Pi image, and it is the whole substance of
     the grant — see "THE COST" below.

LANE. `unaos/crates/kernel/src/drivers/xhci/mod.rs` is rmbp's lane (CLAUDE.md §Worktrees).
This hunk is therefore delivered as a PATCH and is NOT committed to that file; the orin branch
`exec-orin15-clickdead3` carries `drivers/xhci/mod.rs` byte-identical to its base
(`git diff 80ed35a4 -- unaos/crates/kernel/src/drivers/xhci/mod.rs` -> 0 lines).

APPLY.  git apply docs/dev/evidence/orin15/CLICKDEAD-xhci.patch
Base: `80ed35a4` (`aarch64/tegra: FOLDFIX — display_tegra.rs tail union …`), the hw-jetson tip.
v1's base was `2de4a58b`; FOLDFIX moved `ptrpoll_witness` from `display_tegra.rs:5111` to
`:5361`, so v1's `display_tegra.rs` hunks no longer sit at their stated offsets. The hunks
below are regenerated against `80ed35a4` and apply there exactly.

The second file, `arch/aarch64/display_tegra.rs`, IS orin's lane, but its hunks ride HERE and
not in the commit for one reason: they name `MOUSE_DUP_DROP_COUNT` and
`MOUSE_NOBUF_DROP_COUNT`, which do not exist until the first file's hunk lands. Splitting them
would leave the branch unbuildable. **Apply both or neither.**

THE MECHANISM, NAMED. Not "a TRB is not re-queued" — a COMPLETION IS CONSUMED AND THE READ IS
NEVER RE-ARMED, and the discard is silent. At `80ed35a4:XhciController::poll_events`, in the
pointer arm guarded by `mouse_dci == Some(endpoint_id)` (mod.rs:4560), the dup-Success guard
fires when `slot.mouse_expect_phys != 0 && param != slot.mouse_expect_phys` (mod.rs:4587).
Inside it, `80ed35a4:XhciController::queue_mouse_read` (mod.rs:14846) is called ONLY under
`param != prev && have_buf` (mod.rs:4593). The complement falls through to the bare `return;`
at mod.rs:4599. That branch:

  * consumes the transfer event (the ring dequeue pointer has already advanced),
  * does NOT call `queue_mouse_read`, so no new interrupt-IN TD is enqueued on the pointer DCI
    and no doorbell rings — the endpoint is left with nothing armed,
  * and prints NOTHING on the flight build, because its only witness,
    `80ed35a4:XhciController::piusb39_witness` (mod.rs:14730-14731), is
    `#[cfg(feature = "usbdebug")]` and `usbdebug` is absent from the Orin flight recipe
    (witness,ehcihid,holocron,tegra,orinclick,tegra_el0,tegrasmp,orinrender,
    desktop_firmware,orinrx,tcuprobe,deskcascade).

Every OTHER exit accounts for itself: `80ed35a4:MOUSE_REARM_COUNT` (mod.rs:2377) on every
`queue_mouse_read`, `80ed35a4:MOUSE_DISCARD_REARM_COUNT` (mod.rs:2380) on the guard's
re-arming exit, `80ed35a4:MOUSE_ERROR_REARM_COUNT` (mod.rs:2385) on the non-halting error
re-arm, and halting completions go to `80ed35a4:XhciController::hid_error_witness`
(mod.rs:14748), which is UNGATED. So this one branch is the whole blind spot, and on the wire
it is INDISTINGUISHABLE from "the controller posted no transfer event at all" — which is
exactly the ambiguity render6 died in (`docs/dev/evidence/orin15/render6-boot1.log`:
`:: MOUSE-1: 1 reports ::` once at line 286 and never again, zero `hid_error_witness` lines,
zero `JD20 — pointer BUTTON`).

THE SPLIT (rmbp condition 1) — WHY ONE COUNTER WAS WRONG.
v1 bumped one atomic on `param == prev || !have_buf`. Those are two different defects whose
repairs point in opposite directions, so a single `dup>0` on the wire would have named a
symptom and not a fix:

  * `param == slot.mouse_prev_phys`, buffer and ring PRESENT — the known Panther-Point
    duplicate Success (XHCI_SPURIOUS_SUCCESS, device 0x1e31) for a TD already consumed. The
    guard skips the re-arm BY DESIGN: a fresh read is supposed to be outstanding already, and
    re-arming would over-arm the interrupt-IN ring (the UI1-MOUSE M2 hazard). If that
    assumption is wrong on this board, the repair is IN THE GUARD — `param == prev` may only
    skip the re-arm when a read is provably outstanding.
  * `slot.mouse_data_buffer` / `slot.mouse_ring` GONE — a teardown or allocation defect. Here
    re-arming would be flatly WRONG: there is nothing to arm. The repair is in the slot's soft
    state (`Slot::reset_soft_state`, mod.rs:2991, and whoever cleared the buffer without
    disarming the endpoint), and it is a class of fault the dup discrimination cannot touch.

v2 therefore gives each its own ungated `pub AtomicU64` and its own verdict:

  `-> DUP-DROP`   = the guard ate a completion it recognised as the known duplicate.
  `-> NOBUF-DROP` = the completion arrived with the buffer/ring already gone.

PRECEDENCE, stated because the two arms can overlap. Reaching the counted exit means
`!(param != prev && have_buf)`, so `!have_buf` and `param == prev` cover it exhaustively but
not disjointly. `!have_buf` is tested FIRST: a duplicate that arrives after the buffer is gone
scores NOBUF, because the missing buffer is the actionable fault and the duplicate is noise on
top of it. The two arms therefore partition the branch, and `dup + nobuf` is its exact total.

WHAT THE PATCH ADDS.
  * `MOUSE_DUP_DROP_COUNT` and `MOUSE_NOBUF_DROP_COUNT` — two ungated `pub AtomicU64`s, folded
    onto the `MOUSE_ERROR_REARM_COUNT` declaration line, bumped on exactly that branch.
  * `dup=` and `nobuf=` fields on the `[ptrpoll]` census line printed by
    `80ed35a4:ptrpoll_witness` (display_tegra.rs:5361), and the `DUP-DROP` / `NOBUF-DROP`
    verdicts.
  * `dup` and `nobuf` folded into the witness's MOVEMENT test
    (`PTRPOLL_LAST.swap(rearm + dup + nobuf)`). Without that, a pipeline being EATEN (rearm
    flat, drops climbing) would be silently mistaken for one that is STARVED and print one
    line for the whole boot.

After this patch the three live sub-mechanisms are separated on a single line:

  `-> DUP-DROP`            = (a1) completions arrive; the guard eats a recognised duplicate.
  `-> NOBUF-DROP`          = (a1) completions arrive; the buffer/ring is gone. Different fix.
  `-> ARMED-NO-COMPLETION` = (a2) dup=0 and nobuf=0; the endpoint posted nothing. Look at EP
                             state, the doorbell and periodic bandwidth, not at the guard.

The patch is DIAGNOSTIC ONLY. It does not change the guard's control flow — the dup hazard is
real and PIUSB-39's discrimination stays exactly as it is. It adds two relaxed `fetch_add`s
(at most ONE of which executes per completion) and two relaxed loads on the census pass. No
lock, no MMIO.

LINE-NEUTRAL IN `xhci/mod.rs` — YES. BYTE-NEUTRAL ON THE PI IMAGE — NO, AND THAT IS THE COST
OF THE GRANT. Two separate claims, both measured, and v1's header was right to separate them.

  * LINE-neutral in `drivers/xhci/mod.rs`, verified. That file is compiled into the Pi's
    `kernel8.img`, and a line added anywhere in it moves every `panic::Location` below
    (MEMORY: "cfg does not protect byte identity"). Both of its hunks are therefore FOLDED
    ONTO EXISTING LINES and both hunk headers read `7,7 -> 7,7`. Position proof, `python3`,
    base `80ed35a4` vs. patched:

        line counts     : old 14917  new 14917  -> EQUAL
        differing lines : [2385, 4599]
        MOUSE_ERROR_REARM_COUNT decl : old [2385]  new [2385]
        MOUSE_DUP_DROP_COUNT decl    : new [2385]
        MOUSE_NOBUF_DROP_COUNT decl  : new [2385]
        guard bare `return;`         : old [4599]
        guard folded `return;`       : new [4599]

    Nothing in that file moves.
  * `arch/aarch64/display_tegra.rs` gains 4 lines (5397 -> 5401) and is NOT line-neutral. Per
    rmbp's note, added lines move every `panic::Location` BELOW their site inside that file —
    so the claim has to be measured, not asserted. Measured here: `ptrpoll_witness` occupies
    `:5361-5397` at `80ed35a4`, i.e. it IS the file tail, so the set of `Location`s below the
    site is EMPTY and nothing pre-existing in that file moves either. The four added lines are
    the two new verdict arms; every other change is folded onto an existing line.
  * BYTE-neutral on `kernel8.img` — it is NOT, and the patch must not pretend otherwise:
        knob-off `./arroyo kernel8` unpatched ->
          d73a8981d65bd24e254567934f0f2d21b3307b4a761408618d576623e2669fb0
        knob-off `./arroyo kernel8` patched   ->
          8ff7c1d1f4e8938d9a29df4a094ecc1fe01684350adeef8a577b13c5eb89dc13
        both 1,254,984 bytes — same SIZE, different CONTENT.
    The reason is deliberate, not accidental, and it is rmbp condition 2:
    `MOUSE_DUP_DROP_COUNT` and `MOUSE_NOBUF_DROP_COUNT` and their `fetch_add`s are UNGATED,
    exactly like the three siblings they join (`80ed35a4:MOUSE_REARM_COUNT`, mod.rs:2373-2385:
    "Bumped unconditionally (cheap relaxed adds); only the knob-gated witness prints"). Gating
    them on `orinclick` would put an orin-named knob in a shared driver, which
    MEMORY:"name by subsystem, not board" forbids; gating them on `usbdebug` would rebuild the
    very blind spot this patch exists to close. So the patch costs every aarch64 image two
    8-byte BSS atomics and one relaxed increment on the pointer-completion path — no new file
    bytes (the size is unchanged), no lock, no MMIO, no control-flow change.

